Third-Party Runtime Notice: SurrealDB Binary (All-in-One Container Image)
========================================================================

The Engram Rust binary (`engram`), Python Hermes plugin (`integrations/hermes/engram`),
SurrealQL migrations (`migrations/`), and specification documentation (`docs/`) are
pure permissive open-source software (Apache-2.0) and do NOT link against any
Business Source License (BSL-1.1) crates. As documented in `docs/spec/00-decisions.md`
(ADR-02 and ADR-16), `engram` communicates with SurrealDB exclusively over a clean-room
HTTP CBOR RPC client (`reqwest` + `ciborium`).

When distributed as the all-in-one container image (`Dockerfile`), the container image
bundles the unmodified official `surreal` server binary from `surrealdb/surrealdb:v3.3.4`,
which is licensed by SurrealDB Ltd under the Business Source License 1.1 (BSL-1.1).

Under SurrealDB's BSL-1.1 Additional Use Grant:
  - Production use of the unmodified `surreal` binary is permitted, provided the combined
    work is not offered to third parties as a hosted or managed Database-as-a-Service
    (DBaaS) that competes with SurrealDB Cloud.
  - Each release version of SurrealDB automatically converts from BSL-1.1 to the
    Apache License 2.0 on the fourth anniversary of its initial release date.
  - Full license text: https://surrealdb.com/license

Users who require a container image containing zero BSL-1.1 binaries may run `engram`
with `ENGRAM_SURREAL_MODE=external` against an externally managed SurrealDB instance
(see `docker-compose.external.yml` and `docs/spec/08-deployment-operations.md`).
